A||N||6||Monkeyman||WTF--My battery's dead!||||8||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||05-01-2000||11:06 AM||||Can a battery die just sitting in a car?  I bought a new Sears Diehard battery for the Grape Ape (still not running) about 3 weeks ago.  I could operate the headlights, door locks, and windows just fine with it.  I haven't done anything to the car (no stereo yet, etc).  I just went out to install my new steering wheel and realized that my battery is dead!  There isn't even enough juice to pop the trunk.  (Good thing I replaced the lock cylinder!)  I was messing with the car the other day and I still had juice left.  What could have gone wrong?  Help!!!!!!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||bad88||05-03-2000||09:56 PM||||did you check your interior lights?  Make sure the headlight switch wasn't all the way up, leaving the interior lights on or the courtesy light switches weren't on.  otherwise you may have a ground somewhere draining the battery like a stereo or something ||||reg||1
Z||000002||86se/lambo||05-07-2000||02:35 AM||||I have had a few batteries go dead on me.. all for various reasons, but here is a great trick to figure out whats causing the drainage. Remove the negative battery cable and connect one end to the - cable and the other to the - battery terminal. If the light is on there is a drain on the system.<BR>Pull a fuse from the fuse box one at a time until the light goes out, this will tell you what circuit it is in. It has always worked great for me.||||reg||6
Z||000003||Phaeton||05-07-2000||03:42 AM||||The battery going dead over a period of days or even less of nonuse is always a sign for me to get a new alternator as it is almost always leaky diodes causing the battery to go dead.<P>I am so sure, I will email you my $.02 if it is not the diodes.<P>That is a stretch since my own Fiero has a bad habit of leaving the trunk light on, but I feel the Force is with me on this one.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Monkeyman||05-07-2000||09:40 AM||||Hopefully it will be going in the shop tomorrow to get an engine swap.  I'll have them check out the alternator while they're under the hood.  Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||05-07-2000||12:54 PM||||I used to blow the fuses for my trunk release until I insulated the trunk light connector at the light and reversed he polarity at the connector.  My kick panel lamps would come on real dim when the fuse blew.  It's been a few years, but I think it was a parallel path powered backward through the kickpanel lamps (I'm not gonna argue hole flow or electron flow here).   <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 05-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-08-2000||10:05 AM||||Capt. Kirk used to talk about a parallel universe, but I don't know anything about a parallel path.  The Grape Ape's going down to the shop in a few minutes, so I guess I'll let them deal with it.  Thanx everyone, but I just don't have time.  100 hours at work and I've only been home for a total of 12 hours since Thursday evening.  Whew!||||reg||1
